  [1mWhat's worth testing[0m (in rough priority):

  1. [1mThe allowlist drops unknown keys.[0m Pass [38;5;153m{ password: "hunter2", reason: "x" }[39m and assert only [38;5;153mreason[39m survives. This
     is [3mthe[0m safety property — if one test exists, this is it.
  2. [1mThe scrub layer redacts[0m [38;5;153mpassword[39m / [38;5;153mtoken[39m / [38;5;153mauthorization[39m keys in a [38;5;153mbeforeSend[39m payload.
  3. [1mNo-op fallback[0m — with [38;5;153mwindow.Sentry[39m undefined, every [38;5;153mlog.*[39m call returns without throwing.
  4. [1m[38;5;153mlog.*[39m never throws[0m, even given circular objects, [38;5;153mundefined[39m, or a DOM-like object.
  5. [1mEnvironment resolution[0m picks [38;5;153mdevelopment[39m for localhost and [38;5;153mproduction[39m otherwise.

  These need a fake Sentry object, not the real SDK — which is a point in favour of the façade design: the vendor is one
  injectable boundary.

  [1mBeyond unit tests[0m, there's one test that proves the actual claim end-to-end: load [38;5;153mindex.html[39m in a headless browser
  with [38;5;153mwindow.Sentry[39m stubbed to record payloads, type a real password into the form, submit, and assert the string never
  appears in anything captured. That's a Playwright-class test — genuinely the most valuable single test here, and also
  the only thing on this list that adds a heavyweight dependency to a repo that currently has zero.

  My recommendation: [1mlint/format + unit tests[0m. Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m runner needs no dependencies at all, which fits
  this repo's character; [38;5;153meslint[39m + [38;5;153mprettier[39m are the JS standard and catch the "logged the whole object by accident"
  class of mistake. I'd take the E2E leak test too if you're willing to accept Playwright, but I won't assume it.
